## 3. Navigation Pending Feedback & Theme Consistency

### 3.1 Pending feedback is per link

- The only supported pending signal is `useLinkStatus` from `next/link`
  (introduced in Next.js 15.3; App Router only). It must be called inside a
  descendant of the `<Link>` it reports on.
- **Forbidden**: `nprogress`, `next-nprogress-bar`, `@bprogress/*`,
  `react-transition-progress`, `@vercel/react-transition-progress`, router-event
  listeners, or any global "progress provider" in `providers.tsx` /
  `layout.tsx`.
- The indicator is always rendered, reserves fixed space, toggles opacity after
  ~100ms, respects `prefers-reduced-motion`, and is `aria-hidden`. Conditionally
  mounting it causes layout shift; appearing instantly causes flicker.
- Route-level fallbacks (`loading.tsx`, `<Suspense>`) are a different layer and
  remain the preferred fix for genuinely slow routes.
- Contract: `.agents/skills/nextjs-dev-setup/references/navigation-pending-guide.md`.

### 3.2 Theme consistency

- One token layer. Components consume semantic tokens; only the token layer
  differs between light and dark. Do not add tokens ad hoc, and do not invent a
  second token file.
- `next-themes` owns the preference. Its `attribute` must equal the selector the
  stylesheet actually uses: `class`, `data-theme`, or `data-mode`. Do not read
  `localStorage` or `matchMedia` in components.
- Never overwrite, rename, or reorder existing token declarations. Append the
  missing dark counterpart only, and follow the stylesheet structure the project
  already uses — do not impose a CSS file layout, token vocabulary, or color
  space of your own.
- The toggle lives once in the app shell, offers light / dark / system, has an
  accessible name, and reads `resolvedTheme` (guarded on mount).
- `theme`/`resolvedTheme` are `undefined` before mount — guard icon rendering and
  keep `suppressHydrationWarning` on `<html>` only when the provider writes the
  attribute client-side.

