## 4. Agent Roster & Delegation

This repository installs four OpenCode agents. The subagents have no tool-level permission blocks: the orchestrator briefs them with an explicit write scope, they run and verify their own work, and the orchestrator audits the diff on return.

| Agent | Mode | Owns | Never touches |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `nextjs-orchestrator` | primary | gates, delegation, plan checklist, approved edge-test corrections | implementation or tests |
| `nextjs-dev` | subagent | components, pages, skeletons, dictionaries, barrel files | tests, test plans, runner configs, manifests |
| `nextjs-test-author` | subagent | `*.test.ts(x)`, `tests/e2e/pages/**`, `test-plan.md` | implementation, dictionaries, edge tests |
| `nextjs-qa-auditor` | subagent | `*.edge.test.tsx`, `tests/e2e/components/**` | implementation, baseline tests |

Rules:

1. **One role per artifact.** No agent writes both implementation and tests.
2. **Failing test means failing code.** Report `code_fixes_required`; never weaken an assertion.
3. **Edge tests are immutable.** Only an explicit user decision changes one, applied by the orchestrator.
4. **Baseline tests are mutable with a reason.** `nextjs-test-author` records every revision.
5. **Only the orchestrator delegates, and subagents do not ask.** A subagent returns `BLOCKED` with the missing input instead of pausing the pipeline.
6. **A brief scopes every spawn.** Role, objective, mode, `write_scope`, `read_scope`, `forbidden`, expected commands, definition of done.
7. **Subagents verify themselves** — they run their own build, lint, type check, and targeted suites and return real command output.
8. **The orchestrator audits the diff** before reporting success: touched paths vs `write_scope`, then re-runs the claimed-green suite.
9. **Command shortcuts**: `/create-nextjs-component` and `/create-nextjs-page` route to `nextjs-orchestrator`.

Full contract: [`.agents/rules/agent-boundaries.md`](./.agents/rules/agent-boundaries.md).

### 4.1 Test Tiers & Isolated Branches

**Test tiers — fast during development, expensive pre-push:**

| Tier | Contents | Runs | Never runs |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Fast | Targeted Jest file(s) for the artifact under test | Every development loop | — |
| Expensive | Playwright component/page E2E, full Jest, `build`, Storybook build | `.husky/pre-push`, CI, explicit user request | During development |

- E2E specs are still **authored** in the same session, statically audited, and
  declared in `deferred_expensive_tests[]`.
- Report them `DEFERRED (pre-push)`; never `PASS`.
- `verify-qa` / `verify-component-create` default to `-Tier fast`; `-Tier full` is
  pre-push/CI only.
- Full contract:
  [`.agents/skills/nextjs-adversarial-qa/references/shared/test-execution-tiers.md`](./.agents/skills/nextjs-adversarial-qa/references/shared/test-execution-tiers.md).

**Isolated branches — one worktree per component/page task:**

- Implementation agents never write in the user's working tree. They work on
  branch `agent/<slug>` at `../<repo>-worktrees/<slug>`.
- Create: `pwsh .agents/skills/nextjs-dev-setup/scripts/agent-worktree.ps1 -Action create -Slug <slug>`
  (or `bash .agents/skills/nextjs-dev-setup/scripts/agent-worktree.sh create <slug>`).
- Merge into `main` with `--no-ff` after the fast gates and write audit pass.
- **Merge conflict → stop.** The helper aborts the merge, keeps the branch, prints
  the conflicting paths, and exits `3`. Ask the user to choose: A) rebase the agent
  branch onto `main`, B) keep `main` and drop the conflicting hunk, C) manual
  resolution. Never `-X ours`/`-X theirs`, never force-push, never rewrite history.
- Agents never push and never use `--no-verify`; the expensive tier is the push gate.
- Full contract:
  [`.agents/skills/nextjs-dev-setup/references/agent-worktree-isolation.md`](./.agents/skills/nextjs-dev-setup/references/agent-worktree-isolation.md).

